# AshGrpc: gRPC Extension for Ash Resources

```elixir
Application.put_env(:ash, :validate_domain_resource_inclusion?, false)
Application.put_env(:ash, :validate_domain_config_inclusion?, false)

Mix.install(
  [
    {:ash_grpc, path: "/data/"},
    {:ash, "~> 3.0"},
    {:grpc, "~> 0.9"},
    {:protobuf, "~> 0.13"},
    {:jason, "~> 1.4"}
  ],
  consolidate_protocols: false
)
```

## What Is AshGrpc?

`AshGrpc` maps Ash actions to [gRPC](https://grpc.io/) methods. Add the extension to a resource, declare which actions to expose, and you get:

1. **A generated `.proto` file** — the canonical contract for your service, derived entirely from the resource and its actions. No hand-authoring proto files.
2. **A `GRPC.Server` implementation** — one callback per method, wired to `AshGrpc.Server.Dispatcher`.
3. **Full introspection** — the method configuration is queryable at compile time.

AshGrpc handles unary, server-streaming (a stream of records), and client-streaming (a batch of inputs) patterns. It also ships interceptors for authentication, trace propagation, and telemetry.

## The Domain: Inventory Units

We'll model a simple inventory service. A Unit has a name, SKU, and quantity. We want unary create / get-by-id / update / delete methods, a server-streaming list for live feeds, and a client-streaming bulk-ingest endpoint.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Inventory.Unit do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Demo.Inventory,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets,
    extensions: [AshGrpc.Resource]

  grpc do
    # Fully-qualified proto service name.
    # The proto package is derived from everything before the last segment.
    service_name "demo.inventory.UnitService"

    # method <dsl_name>, action: <ash_action_name>, [options]
    method :create_unit,  action: :create
    method :get_unit,     action: :read,    get?: true          # returns a single record
    method :list_units,   action: :read,    server_stream: true # streams one record per message
    method :update_unit,  action: :update
    method :delete_unit,  action: :destroy
    method :bulk_ingest,  action: :create,  client_stream: true # receives a stream of inputs
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id

    attribute :name,     :string,  allow_nil?: false, public?: true
    attribute :sku,      :string,  allow_nil?: false, public?: true
    attribute :quantity, :integer, default: 0,        public?: true
    attribute :active,   :boolean, default: true,     public?: true

    create_timestamp :inserted_at
    update_timestamp :updated_at
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read, :destroy]

    create :create do
      accept [:name, :sku, :quantity, :active]
    end

    update :update do
      accept [:name, :quantity, :active]
    end
  end
end

defmodule Demo.Inventory do
  use Ash.Domain, validate_config_inclusion?: false

  resources do
    resource Demo.Inventory.Unit
  end
end
```

## Introspection

`AshGrpc.Resource.Info` exposes the full gRPC configuration at compile time — no server or proto files needed.

```elixir
# All declared methods
AshGrpc.Resource.Info.methods(Demo.Inventory.Unit)
```

```elixir
# Look up by DSL name
AshGrpc.Resource.Info.method(Demo.Inventory.Unit, :create_unit)
```

```elixir
# Look up by wire RPC name (PascalCase of the method name)
AshGrpc.Resource.Info.method_by_rpc(Demo.Inventory.Unit, "GetUnit")
```

```elixir
# Resolved service name
AshGrpc.Resource.Info.service_name!(Demo.Inventory.Unit)
```

```elixir
# Proto package — derived from service_name if not set explicitly
AshGrpc.Resource.Info.package!(Demo.Inventory.Unit)
```

## Proto Generation

This runs entirely in the notebook — no NATS server, no protoc, no S3. `AshGrpc.Proto.Generator.generate/1` inspects the resource and emits a complete proto3 definition.

```elixir
proto = AshGrpc.Proto.Generator.generate(Demo.Inventory.Unit)
IO.puts(proto)
```

### What the generator derives

| Element            | Source                                                                    |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `package`          | `service_name` minus the last segment                                     |
| `rpc` names        | `rpc_name` (default: PascalCase of the DSL method name)                   |
| Request fields     | Accepted attributes for the action + PK fields for get/update/destroy     |
| Response fields    | Public resource attributes (optionally restricted by `fields:`)           |
| List response      | Wraps the record message in `repeated … records = 1;`                   |
| `stream` keyword   | Added when `server_stream: true` or `client_stream: true`                 |
| Well-known imports | `google/protobuf/timestamp.proto` added automatically for datetime fields |

### A resource with custom rpc_name, datetime, and doc comments

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Telemetry.Reading do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Demo.Telemetry,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets,
    extensions: [AshGrpc.Resource]

  grpc do
    service_name "demo.telemetry.ReadingService"

    # Override the default wire name ("Ingest" instead of "Ingest").
    method :ingest, action: :create,
      rpc_name: "Ingest",
      description: "Record a new sensor reading."

    # Server-streaming: each matching record is sent as a separate message.
    method :stream_readings, action: :read, server_stream: true,
      description: "Stream all readings matching the filter."
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id
    attribute :sensor_id,   :string,            public?: true
    attribute :value,       :float,             public?: true
    attribute :recorded_at, :utc_datetime_usec, public?: true
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]
    create :create do
      accept [:sensor_id, :value, :recorded_at]
    end
  end
end

defmodule Demo.Telemetry do
  use Ash.Domain, validate_config_inclusion?: false
  resources do
    resource Demo.Telemetry.Reading
  end
end

IO.puts(AshGrpc.Proto.Generator.generate(Demo.Telemetry.Reading))
```

Notice that `recorded_at` (`Ash.Type.UtcDatetimeUsec`) maps to `google.protobuf.Timestamp` and the import is added automatically.

### Type mapping reference

```elixir
alias AshGrpc.Proto.TypeMapping

# We simulate what the generator does for common Ash types.
# proto_type/1 takes %{type: …, constraints: …} and returns {proto_type, repeated?}.
ash_types = [
  Ash.Type.String,
  Ash.Type.CiString,
  Ash.Type.Integer,
  Ash.Type.Float,
  Ash.Type.Boolean,
  Ash.Type.UUID,
  Ash.Type.Decimal,
  Ash.Type.UtcDatetime,
  Ash.Type.UtcDatetimeUsec,
  Ash.Type.Date,
  Ash.Type.Time,
  Ash.Type.Map,
  Ash.Type.Binary
]

for type <- ash_types do
  {proto_type, repeated?} = TypeMapping.proto_type(%{type: type, constraints: []})
  r = if repeated?, do: "repeated ", else: ""
  "#{inspect(type)} → #{r}#{proto_type}"
end
```

```elixir
# Arrays map to repeated fields
{proto_type, repeated?} =
  TypeMapping.proto_type(%{type: {:array, Ash.Type.String}, constraints: [items: []]})

"Array of strings → repeated? #{repeated?}, type: #{proto_type}"
```

## The Server Module

After running `protoc` on the generated `.proto` file you get a `GRPC.Service` module (e.g. `Demo.Inventory.UnitService.Service`). Wire it to the Ash dispatcher with `AshGrpc.Server`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Inventory.UnitServer do
  use AshGrpc.Server,
    resource: Demo.Inventory.Unit,
    service:  Demo.Inventory.UnitService.Service
end
```

`AshGrpc.Server` expands to one function per method. For unary methods:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
def create_unit(request, stream) do
  case AshGrpc.Server.Dispatcher.dispatch(
    Demo.Inventory.Unit, :create_unit, request, stream
  ) do
    {:ok, reply} -> reply
    {:error, %GRPC.RPCError{} = err} -> raise err
  end
end
```

For server-streaming methods (`:list_units`, `:stream_readings`):

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
def list_units(request, stream) do
  case AshGrpc.Server.Dispatcher.dispatch(
    Demo.Inventory.Unit, :list_units, request, stream
  ) do
    {:ok, _stream} -> :ok      # replies were sent via GRPC.Server.send_reply/2
    {:error, err}  -> raise err
  end
end
```

## The Dispatcher

`AshGrpc.Server.Dispatcher` is the runtime engine. For each incoming RPC it:

1. Looks up the method configuration from the resource
2. **Decodes** the protobuf request struct into a plain Ash input map (`AshGrpc.Codec.decode_request/1`)
3. Reads actor and tenant from `stream.local` (populated by the Context interceptor)
4. Runs the Ash action
5. **Encodes** the result back into the response message struct (`AshGrpc.Codec.encode_response/3`)

### Codec walkthrough

```elixir
alias AshGrpc.Codec

# decode_request strips struct metadata and normalises values.
# Google.Protobuf.Timestamp → DateTime; repeated fields → lists.
# Without a real proto module we'll simulate the input:
simulated_request = %{name: "Widget A", sku: "WGT-001", quantity: 100, active: true}
Codec.decode_request(simulated_request)
```

```elixir
# encode_value converts Ash-native types to proto-friendly scalars:
# DateTime → Google.Protobuf.Timestamp
# Decimal  → string
# atom     → string (non-boolean)
# map      → JSON string

examples = [
  ~U[2026-06-16 12:00:00Z],
  Decimal.new("19.99"),
  :active,
  %{"key" => "value"}
]

for v <- examples do
  encoded = apply(Codec, :encode_value, [v])
  "#{inspect(v)} → #{inspect(encoded)}"
end
```

### Error mapping

Ash error classes map to gRPC status codes:

```elixir
# Ash.Error.Invalid      → GRPC.Status.invalid_argument()  (3)
# Ash.Error.Forbidden    → GRPC.Status.permission_denied()  (7)
# Ash.Error.Query.NotFound → GRPC.Status.not_found()        (5)
# anything else          → GRPC.Status.internal()           (13)

# get? reads that return nil reply with NOT_FOUND:
# {:error, %GRPC.RPCError{status: 5, message: "not found"}}
:ok
```

## Streaming

### Server-streaming

For `server_stream: true` methods the dispatcher uses `Ash.stream!/2` (which paginates under the hood) and sends each record as a separate reply via `GRPC.Server.send_reply/2`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# Each record in the result set arrives as a separate gRPC message.
# The client iterates with Enum.to_list/1 or Stream.each/2.
{:ok, stream} = Demo.Inventory.UnitService.Stub.list_units(channel, %{})
Enum.each(stream, fn {:ok, unit} -> IO.inspect(unit) end)
```

### Client-streaming

For `client_stream: true` methods (like `:bulk_ingest`) the dispatcher receives an enumerable of request messages. For create actions it delegates to `Ash.bulk_create/4`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
inputs = Stream.map(csv_rows, &%Demo.Inventory.IngestRequest{name: &1.name, sku: &1.sku})
{:ok, result} = Demo.Inventory.UnitService.Stub.bulk_ingest(channel, inputs)
```

## Interceptors

### Server side

**`AshGrpc.Interceptors.Context`** — resolves actor and tenant from request headers, stashes them in `stream.local`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# In your GRPC.Server endpoint:
intercept AshGrpc.Interceptors.Context,
  actor:  {MyApp.Auth,    :actor_from_token},
  tenant: {MyApp.Tenancy, :from_headers}
# Each MFA receives the headers map and returns the value (or nil).
```

**`AshGrpc.Interceptors.Telemetry`** — wraps each RPC call with telemetry events:

```elixir
# Events emitted:
events = [
  {[:ash_grpc, :rpc, :start],     "system_time", "method + service name"},
  {[:ash_grpc, :rpc, :stop],      "duration",    "method + service name"},
  {[:ash_grpc, :rpc, :exception], "duration",    "method + service + kind + status"}
]

for {event, measurement, metadata} <- events do
  "#{inspect(event)} — measurements: #{measurement}, metadata: #{metadata}"
end
```

### Client side

**`AshGrpc.Interceptors.Client.Auth`** — injects a bearer token into outbound headers:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# Per-connection:
GRPC.Stub.connect("localhost:50051",
  interceptors: [{AshGrpc.Interceptors.Client.Auth, token: my_jwt}])

# Or per-process (useful in tests):
Process.put(:ash_grpc_token, my_jwt)
```

**`AshGrpc.Interceptors.Client.Trace`** — W3C trace context propagation + round-trip telemetry:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# Requires opentelemetry_api (optional dep).
# Injects traceparent / tracestate headers when the OTel propagator is loaded.
# Emits [:ash_grpc, :client, :rpc] with duration and :ok / :error tag.
GRPC.Stub.connect("localhost:50051",
  interceptors: [AshGrpc.Interceptors.Client.Trace])
```

## Mix Task: gen_proto

Generate proto files for an entire domain (or specific resources) and write them to disk:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# Scan a domain
mix ash_grpc.gen_proto --domain Demo.Inventory --out priv/protos

# Multiple domains
mix ash_grpc.gen_proto \
  --domain Demo.Inventory \
  --domain Demo.Telemetry \
  --out priv/protos

# A single resource
mix ash_grpc.gen_proto --resource Demo.Inventory.Unit --out priv/protos
```

After generating, compile with `protoc` to produce Elixir message and service modules:

```bash
protoc --elixir_out=plugins=grpc:./lib priv/protos/unit.proto
```

## Full Lifecycle

```elixir
# Let's review the complete generated proto for both resources one more time:
IO.puts("=== Inventory ===")
IO.puts(AshGrpc.Proto.Generator.generate(Demo.Inventory.Unit))

IO.puts("=== Telemetry ===")
IO.puts(AshGrpc.Proto.Generator.generate(Demo.Telemetry.Reading))
```

The workflow from zero to serving gRPC traffic:

```
1. Add extensions: [AshGrpc.Resource] to your resource
2. Declare methods in a grpc do … end block
3. mix ash_grpc.gen_proto --domain MyApp.Domain --out priv/protos
4. protoc --elixir_out=plugins=grpc:./lib priv/protos/*.proto
5. defmodule MyApp.UnitServer do
     use AshGrpc.Server, resource: MyApp.Unit, service: MyApp.UnitService.Service
   end
6. {GRPC.Server.Supervisor,
     endpoint: MyApp.GrpcEndpoint, port: 50051, start_server: true}
```

## Summary

| Concept               | Where it lives                                                   |
| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Method declarations   | `grpc do … end` on the resource                                |
| Proto source of truth | `AshGrpc.Proto.Generator` + `mix ash_grpc.gen_proto`             |
| Type mapping          | `AshGrpc.Proto.TypeMapping` (Ash types → proto scalars)        |
| Runtime dispatch      | `AshGrpc.Server.Dispatcher`                                      |
| Encode / decode       | `AshGrpc.Codec`                                                  |
| Server wiring         | `use AshGrpc.Server, resource: …, service: …`                |
| Auth                  | `AshGrpc.Interceptors.Context` (server) + `Client.Auth` (client) |
| Tracing               | `AshGrpc.Interceptors.Client.Trace`                              |
| Telemetry             | `AshGrpc.Interceptors.Telemetry`                                 |
| Code generation       | `mix ash_grpc.gen_proto`                                         |

The Ash resource is the single source of truth. Proto files are derived, not authored. Add the extension, run the generator, wire the server — gRPC comes for free.
